    button:hover { border-color: var(--accent); }
    pre {
      margin: 0;
      padding: 16px;
      overflow: auto;
      white-space: pre-wrap;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
      font: 13px/1.45 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, "Liberation Mono", monospace;
    }
    .hidden { display: none; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <main>
    <section id="home">
      <div class="intro">
        <h1>Work Leaf Three-Feature Benchmarks</h1>
        <p>This dashboard reads <code>bench-results</code> and compares the same three requested features across benchmark modes.</p>
        <p><strong>Work Leaf</strong> rows run the orchestrator release under test against the fixed base commit. There are many Work Leaf rows because every new candidate release can be benchmarked and saved.</p>
        <p><strong>Sequential</strong> runs direct Codex on request 1, review/fix/commit, then request 2, then request 3, followed by a final linearization pass. <strong>Worktree</strong> runs the direct Codex patch/review loops in parallel worktrees and then asks a direct Codex linearizer to integrate the reviewed branches.</p>
        <p>To run a saved Work Leaf benchmark output, use <code>./start --bench</code> and select one of the saved artifact directories.</p>
        <p>The benchmark commands are intentionally not perfectly written so we can see whether agents can still produce the intended feature. The only tightly written command is the <code>/&lt;command&gt;</code> feature request. As of the latest recorded bench, <span id="latest-reference">loading...</span>, no recorded output has implemented that request fully correctly: not sequential, not worktree, and not any saved Work Leaf run.</p>
      </div>
      <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="variance-title">
